// /itineraries/ — Itineraries hub PILLAR (Planning Authority phase).
// Cornerstone: "The Ultimate Dinosaur Country Itinerary Guide."
// Follows the fishing/hiking/things-to-do pillar pattern: content written
// directly as Astro markup + Article/FAQPage JSON-LD. This is the site's
// trip-PLANNING headquarters — it does not duplicate the destination or
// activity guides, it teaches visitors how to COMBINE them into a trip and
// links out to each cornerstone. No HubIndex: `itineraries` is a standalone
// collection (day-by-day itinerary spokes via ItineraryLayout), not a slug
// in the hub registry, so there is nothing to auto-list yet — future
// itinerary spokes are seeded, not built here.
//
// Factual discipline (per editorial standards): the only hard numbers stated
// are ones already fixed sitewide — the $349/machine, up-to-2-riders, 3-hour
// UTV tour and (435) 219-9447; the ~3-hour Salt Lake City drive; Kings Peak
// at 13,528 ft; and Vernal sitting above 5,000 ft. Every changing or
// site-specific detail — exact drive times, hours, seasons, road and facility
// status, lodging, fees — is described by character and marked VERIFY WITH
// OFFICIAL SOURCE. No lodging brands, prices, or schedules are invented.
// Reachability: linked in-body from the Things-to-Do cornerstone (nav-reachable).
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/layout/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/layout/Footer.astro';
import Seo from '../../components/seo/Seo.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/layout/Breadcrumbs.astro';

const schema = `{
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@graph": [
      {
        "@type": "Article",
        "headline": "The Ultimate Dinosaur Country Itinerary Guide",
        "description": "A local guide's trip-planning headquarters for Vernal and Dinosaur Country — how to turn one, two, or three days into the perfect itinerary for families, adventurers, photographers, anglers, campers, and road-trippers.",
        "author": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "Adventure Tours Vernal"},
        "publisher": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "Adventure Tours Vernal", "logo": {"@type": "ImageObject", "url": "https://adventuretoursvernal.com/images/logo.webp"}},
        "datePublished": "2026-07-11",
        "dateModified": "2026-07-11",
        "mainEntityOfPage": "https://adventuretoursvernal.com/itineraries/"
      },
      {
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "mainEntity": [
          {"@type": "Question", "name": "How many days do you need in Vernal and Dinosaur Country?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Two to three days is the sweet spot. One day is enough for a single headline — Dinosaur National Monument or a guided UTV tour — but forces hard choices. A two-day weekend covers the essentials without rushing: dinosaurs and rock art one day, backcountry adventure and the state parks the next. A third day opens up the bigger excursions — Flaming Gorge, the High Uintas, a fishing or camping trip. Longer trips let you go deep on one theme. Match the length to your interests and your travel time, and always confirm current hours, seasons, and access before you lock a plan."}},
          {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is the best one-day itinerary near Vernal?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "With a single day, pick one anchor and build a half-day around it. The classic is Dinosaur National Monument in the morning — the Quarry and the Cub Creek petroglyph drive — then a Vernal lunch and an easy afternoon at a state park or museum. The adventure version flips it: a morning guided UTV tour into the canyons, then the monument or the Utah Field House in the afternoon. Resist the urge to cram two big destinations into one day; the region rewards depth over box-ticking. Verify current hours and seasonal access before you go."}},
          {"@type": "Question", "name": "Should I stay in Vernal or move between hotels?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "For almost every trip, base in Vernal and take day trips out. The region is built like a wheel — Vernal is the hub, and the monument, state parks, Flaming Gorge, and forest are all within day-trip range, so a single basecamp saves you packing and re-checking-in every night. A moving trip only makes sense on a longer road trip that continues on to other regions, or a dedicated backcountry camping trip where the campground is the point. Confirm current lodging and campground availability with the providers before you commit."}},
          {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is the best time of year for a Dinosaur Country trip?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Late spring and early fall are the region-wide sweet spots — comfortable temperatures, active wildlife, and thinner crowds. Summer is fully open and best for water and high-country trips, but the desert is hot, so plan early starts. Fall brings gold aspens and quiet trails but a closing window up high. Winter is cold and quiet, with some seasonal roads and facilities closed, and works best as a low-desert and town-based trip. Match your itinerary to the season: desert and reservoirs in spring and fall, the High Uintas in summer. Verify seasonal openings before planning around a date."}},
          {"@type": "Question", "name": "Is Vernal good for a family trip?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Exceptionally. The headline attractions are things kids actually care about — real dinosaur fossils in the rock, footprints they can stand beside, canyons, and boats — and most involve only short walks. A great family itinerary keeps drives short, builds in water and downtime, saves the exposed desert for cool mornings, and lets kids ride along on a guided UTV tour as passengers. Two or three relaxed days beat one rushed one. Confirm age policies, hours, and facilities with each provider before you go."}},
          {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can you do Vernal as a weekend trip from Salt Lake City?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes — it is one of the best weekend escapes from the Wasatch Front. Vernal is roughly a three-hour drive east of Salt Lake City on US-40 over the Uinta Mountains, close enough to leave after work or early on a Saturday and get two full days on the ground. Base in Vernal, anchor the weekend with a guided UTV tour and Dinosaur National Monument, and drive home Sunday evening. See our Salt Lake City to Vernal travel guide for the route and timing, and verify current road and weather conditions over the passes before you leave."}},
          {"@type": "Question", "name": "What should I book first when planning a Vernal itinerary?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Book the anchor activities first and build the flexible sights around them. A guided UTV tour has set departure times, so reserve it first — especially in summer and on weekends — and treat it as the fixed point of that day. Do the same for any river trip, lodging, or campground, which sell out in peak season. The monument, state parks, museums, and scenic drives are flexible and slot in around your anchors. Reserve early and confirm current availability directly with each provider."}},
          {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is the best itinerary for adventure travelers?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Adventure travelers should build the trip around backcountry access and elevation. Anchor a day on a guided UTV tour into canyon country you cannot reach from a paved road, add a High Uintas day hike or a stretch of alpine trail, and layer in fishing, paddling, or a camping night to sleep close to the action. In summer, chase cool air up high; in the shoulder seasons, work the desert canyons. This is a big, remote, high-elevation region — carry water and a map, tell someone your plan, and verify trail, road, and permit status with the managing agency first."}},
          {"@type": "Question", "name": "How do I plan the perfect trip to Dinosaur Country?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Start with three questions: how long do you have, who are you traveling with, and what season is it. Pick a trip length, choose a theme that fits your group — family, adventure, photography, fishing, camping, scenic driving, or hiking — and base in Vernal so every destination is a day trip. Book your anchor activities first, match the elevation to the season, pack for high-desert extremes, and read each destination's dedicated guide for the details. Confirm current hours, fees, seasons, and access before you finalize, and leave room to slow down and enjoy it."}}
        ]
      }
    ]
  }`;
